# The macOS artifacts, cross-produced from a Linux container.
#
# This is NOT an image anybody installs. `Containerfile` builds the Linux image
# — the unit of install for a box that takes containers (README "As a container
# image"). This file builds two FILES: the `aarch64-apple-darwin` `litany` and
# the `bz` at the pin, carried out of a scratch layer so `create`/`cp` can lift
# them onto the host. Nothing here is ever pushed, and the runtime reasoning
# that governs the other file — what the engine execs, what mounts where — has
# no meaning for an artifact.
#
# BOTH BINARIES, for the reason the image ships both: `bz` is not optional, the
# install table says every route needs it, litany resolves it on PATH and a
# load-time guard rejects any `bz` whose version differs from the pin. A mac
# artifact that was only the engine would be an install route that cannot
# answer a prompt.
#
# WHY IT EXISTS. A mac binary that comes off somebody's laptop is a binary
# nobody can reproduce and nothing gates. This puts it on the same line as the
# Linux image: the same digest-pinned base, the same toolchain pin read out of
# `rust-toolchain.toml`, the same `--locked` dependency answer the gate judged,
# and the same `brazen` pin read out of `Cargo.toml`.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE TOOLCHAIN IS `zig cc`, AND osxcross IS REFUSED. This is the load-bearing
# decision of the file and it was made on licence, not on taste.
#
# osxcross cross-compiles for macOS by driving Apple's own SDK, which it does
# not ship and cannot ship. The Xcode and Apple SDKs Agreement forbids the
# arrangement twice over, and either clause alone would be enough:
#
#   2.7  "The grants set forth in this Agreement do not permit You to, and You
#         agree not to, install, use or run the Apple Software or Apple
#         Services on any non-Apple-branded computer or device, or to enable
#         others to do so. ... You agree not to rent, lease, lend, upload to or
#         host on any website or server, sell, redistribute, or sublicense the
#         Apple Software and Apple Services, in whole or in part, or to enable
#         others to do so."
#
#   2.5  "You may not alter the Apple Software or Services in any way in such
#         copy, e.g., You are expressly prohibited from separately using the
#         Apple SDKs or attempting to run any part of the Apple Software on
#         non-Apple-branded hardware."
#
# The first says the SDK may never sit in this repository nor in any layer that
# is published; the second says an operator-supplied SDK, mounted at build time
# and never committed, is ALSO outside the grant, because the builder is not
# Apple-branded hardware. So this is not a case of holding the SDK at arm's
# length with a build argument — the whole arm is refused, and a Containerfile
# that took an SDK path would be inviting the operator into a term they cannot
# satisfy on a Linux box.
#
# zig acquires nothing from Apple. It ships ONE darwin stub of its own,
# `lib/libc/darwin/libSystem.tbd`, in its own distribution and under its own
# licence; no Apple agreement is accepted anywhere on this path.
#
# THE LIMIT THAT COMES WITH IT: zig ships libSystem and NO framework stubs —
# not CoreFoundation, not AppKit, and no `libobjc`. A crate graph that links
# only libSystem crosses cleanly; one that links any Apple framework fails at
# the link step with "unable to find framework", and there is no lawful way to
# supply the frameworks on a Linux builder. This graph had exactly one such
# edge and it is gone: `chrono`'s `clock` feature pulled `iana-time-zone`,
# which links CoreFoundation on darwin, and this crate uses `Utc` only, so the
# feature is `now` and five crates left the lockfile with it. That line in
# `Cargo.toml` is load-bearing for this file — widening it back to `clock` un-
# ports the mac build.
#
# AND IT IS A C TOOLCHAIN, DELIBERATELY. The house posture bans the openssl and
# aws-lc classes to stop a C toolchain arriving IMPLICITLY, through a
# dependency edge nobody reviewed. This one arrives explicitly, in a file that
# argues for it, pinned by version and sha256, in a build stage that is
# discarded. The posture is against the accident, not against the compiler.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Stage 1 — build. The same digest-pinned base as `Containerfile`, so the mac
# artifacts and the Linux image are compiled by one rustc.
FROM docker.io/library/rust:1.95.0-alpine3.22@sha256:064dfc925d68d1a63f4fd2871bd7dc6e6ea56692989a487185855d62885d90aa AS build

# `musl-dev` for the host side of the build scripts that compile C; `curl`,
# `tar` and `xz` to fetch and unpack the pinned zig.
RUN apk add --no-cache musl-dev curl tar xz

# The toolchain pin has ONE home — rust-toolchain.toml — and the `FROM` line
# above is a second statement of the same fact, so it can drift. This is the
# same check `Containerfile` runs, for the same reason, and it is copied to
# /pin rather than into the build directory because a rust-toolchain.toml in
# the working directory sends every later cargo through rustup's shim, which
# would try to DOWNLOAD the toolchain into an image that already has it.
COPY rust-toolchain.toml /pin/rust-toolchain.toml
RUN set -eu; \
    pin=$(sed -n 's/^channel *= *"\([^"]*\)".*/\1/p' /pin/rust-toolchain.toml); \
    have=$(rustc --version | cut -d' ' -f2); \
    if [ "$pin" != "$have" ]; then \
      echo "Containerfile.mac: base image rustc $have, rust-toolchain.toml pins $pin" >&2; \
      echo "  bump the FROM tag and its digest in lockstep with the pin" >&2; \
      exit 1; \
    fi

# zig, by version AND by sha256. The version alone is not a pin: ziglang.org
# serves one tarball per release and a checksum is the only thing that says the
# bytes did not change under the name. The linux-x86_64 build is statically
# linked, so it runs on this musl base with no interpreter and no libc match.
ARG ZIG_VERSION=0.16.0
ARG ZIG_SHA256=70e49664a74374b48b51e6f3fdfbf437f6395d42509050588bd49abe52ba3d00
RUN set -eu; \
    cd /tmp; \
    tarball="zig-x86_64-linux-${ZIG_VERSION}.tar.xz"; \
    curl -fsSLO "https://ziglang.org/download/${ZIG_VERSION}/${tarball}"; \
    echo "${ZIG_SHA256}  ${tarball}" | sha256sum -c -; \
    tar -xJf "${tarball}" -C /opt; \
    mv "/opt/zig-x86_64-linux-${ZIG_VERSION}" /opt/zig; \
    ln -s /opt/zig/zig /usr/local/bin/zig; \
    rm -f "${tarball}"; \
    zig version

# cargo-zigbuild is what makes `zig cc` usable as rustc's linker: rustc emits
# darwin linker flags zig does not accept, and this filters them. Exact version
# and `--locked`, for the reason every other build step here is locked.
ARG ZIGBUILD_VERSION=0.23.3
RUN cargo install cargo-zigbuild --version "${ZIGBUILD_VERSION}" --locked

# The target's `std`, from the same pinned toolchain. Cross-compiling needs no
# Apple anything for this half — rustc's macOS std is distributed by rust-lang.
RUN rustup target add aarch64-apple-darwin

WORKDIR /src

# COPY by name, the same set the Linux build stage takes: the embedded asset
# trees are compiled into the binary, so they are inputs here too.
COPY Cargo.toml Cargo.lock ./
COPY crates ./crates
COPY src ./src
COPY template ./template
COPY schemas ./schemas
COPY skills ./skills
COPY install ./install

# Only the `litany` binary, as in the Linux build: `agent-eval` and
# `litany-eval-agent` are repo-side evaluation tooling with no meaning on a
# deployed box.
#
# THE MACOS FLOOR IS NOT ASKED FOR, IT IS READ BACK. rustc passes
# `-mmacosx-version-min` and the pinned zig ignores it, stamping its own darwin
# minimum into LC_BUILD_VERSION; cargo-zigbuild's `aarch64-apple-darwin.<ver>`
# target syntax does not survive this zig either (it forms a target query zig
# rejects). So the floor is a property of the pinned pair, not a setting, and
# `scripts/mac-verify.sh` prints what the artifact actually carries. Do not
# write the floor into a README from memory — read it off the binary.
RUN cargo zigbuild --release --locked --target aarch64-apple-darwin --bin litany

# The adapter, at the pin, cross-linked the same way. The pin is READ from
# `Cargo.toml`'s `brazen = "="` line, which is its one home — not typed here,
# because a version typed into a second file is a version that drifts.
#
# `cargo-zigbuild install` and not `cargo zigbuild install`: cargo's subcommand
# dispatch turns `cargo zigbuild ...` into `cargo-zigbuild zigbuild ...`, which
# has no `install` verb. The binary is invoked directly to reach it.
RUN set -eu; \
    pin=$(sed -n 's/^brazen = "=\([^"]*\)"$/\1/p' Cargo.toml); \
    test -n "$pin" || { echo 'Containerfile.mac: no `brazen = "=<version>"` line in Cargo.toml' >&2; exit 1; }; \
    cargo-zigbuild install brazen --version "=$pin" --locked \
      --target aarch64-apple-darwin --root /adapter

# Stage 2 — the carrier. Two files, nothing else: `podman create` + `podman cp`
# lifts them out without running anything, and there is no runtime layer to
# reason about because nothing here is ever run. It is not `FROM alpine` for
# the same reason it is not pushed: these are files in a wrapper, not an
# install.
FROM scratch
COPY --from=build /src/target/aarch64-apple-darwin/release/litany /litany
COPY --from=build /adapter/bin/bz /bz
